<Docs
  title="Quota ledger"
  description="The local record behind the quota-aware agent pool, its persistence, and the remaining design contract."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Slot persistence is implemented">
    The pool slot record and its local database table now exist. The quota ledger, priority
    selection, idle release and pool UI are still planned, so the agent pool is not available in
    the current build. This page records the agreed contract so the implementation, tests and user
    guide cannot drift.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    <Badge tone="warning" variant="subtle">planned</Badge> The pool work is split into slot
    persistence, the quota ledger, priority selection, idle release and the pool UI. Slot
    persistence is implemented and tested; the other four items are not checked off. The
    user-facing promise is on the <a href="/docs/users/agent-pool">agent pool</a> page; this page is
    for the implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slot">The slot record</h2>
  <p>
    A slot is an installed agent plus one provider/model plus the credential or account it uses. A
    persisted slot needs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The user's priority order and the provider/model metadata.</li>
    <li>A local usage ledger, reset window, cooldown state and last-used timestamp.</li>
    <li>A reference to the credential or account. Never copy the credential into the slot, logs or database.</li>
    <li>An enabled/disabled state and the last error or health signal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ledger">The ledger</h2>
  <p>
    The ledger is local. It can be seeded from a catalog snapshot when one is available, updated from
    provider rate-limit or quota responses, and corrected by the user. It is not a provider billing
    record and must not claim a limit it cannot observe.
  </p>
  <p>
    A quota response is evidence for a slot, not a global fact. Preserve the source and timestamp so
    a later reset or correction can be explained. If the provider reports no capacity, record
    <strong>unknown</strong>; never translate that to free or unlimited.
  </p>

  <h2 id="availability">Availability</h2>
  <p>
    Before selection, a slot must answer whether it can accept work at the current time. The
    implementation needs states for available, exhausted, cooling down, disabled, unhealthy and
    unknown. Exhausted and cooling-down slots are skipped with a visible reason; unknown capacity is
    not treated as available.
  </p>

  <h2 id="selection">Selection</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Try the user's preferred healthy slot.</li>
    <li>If it cannot accept work, try the next slot in priority order.</li>
    <li>An explicit task override wins over the order.</li>
    <li>Record the chosen slot and every skipped higher-priority slot with its reason.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Selection must be atomic across concurrent tasks. The transcript or task header names the chosen
    slot, so a fallback is never silent. A slot that is exhausted for one provider/model must not
    block an unrelated slot.
  </p>

  <h2 id="release">Idle release</h2>
  <p>
    After a turn settles, keep the slot reserved for a configurable idle timeout (default 15
    minutes). Release it after that timeout so another task can use it. Never release during a
    running turn or a pending approval.
  </p>
  <p>
    Releasing a reservation must not destroy a resumable agent session. If the adapter cannot resume,
    the task says so and selects another slot for the next turn. Tests need a follow-up after release,
    a slot claimed in the meantime, cancellation and restart recovery.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">Corrections and privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The user can edit a limit or reset time, clear a cooldown, reorder slots, disable a slot and force
    a slot for one turn. The pool UI shows provider/model, priority, availability, known limit/reset,
    current task, last error and last-used time. It must not display credentials or invent a quota.
  </p>
  <p>
    The catalog is advisory. A local ledger and the user's correction are authoritative for local
    selection; a stale catalog snapshot must not silently overwrite them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tests">Planned tests</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Deterministic clock tests for reset windows, provider errors, clock changes and user corrections.</li>
    <li>Simultaneous starts, exhaustion, cooldown, provider failure and manual override.</li>
    <li>Idle release, a follow-up after release, a slot claimed meanwhile, cancellation and restart recovery.</li>
    <li>Credential redaction in records, logs and diagnostics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Until those tests exist, the agent pool remains a next-step item. See the
    <a href="/docs/contributors/test-fixtures">test fixtures</a> page for the fixture rules that apply
    when the work lands.
  </p>
</Docs>
